And today we're going to dive further into
the introduction to nuclear chemistry,

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.160
specifically looking at half life.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:14.189
So what this is all about is going
off of what our last video was all

00:00:14.189 --> 00:00:17.919
about, which was all the different
types of nuclear radiation.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:22.030
So whenever you have an unstable
nuclei, let's say like the one here

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:26.430
with all these little protons and
neutrons, It will decay, meaning it

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:30.230
could go through let's say a gamma
radiation where it just releases

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:35.489
energy, maybe it releases a positron,
a beta particle, an alpha particle.

00:00:35.739 --> 00:00:38.510
So if you're trying to interested
in balancing those reactions,

00:00:38.629 --> 00:00:39.779
refer to my other video.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.429
This one's just on half life.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.240
So when we have radioactive decay.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.150
What exactly is happening?

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.720
Well, it takes so much time
for each one of these radio

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.919
isotopes to decay and break down.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:58.650
Now, every time, whenever we have one
of these, it goes through a half life.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.659
So, what the whole idea
of a half life is...

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:06.800
It's whenever we have a radioisotope
and it's the time it takes for it to,

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:12.259
for it to decay by exactly one half
of its original amount, meaning that

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:17.789
if I have, let's say a sample here
and it goes through exactly one half

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:21.810
life, so we'll just say one T one half.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:25.280
So when that happens, it's
going to decay by half.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:30.200
So like, as we have on this original
portion here, this is a hundred percent.

00:01:30.714 --> 00:01:35.144
Then when it goes through one half
life, it's gonna go down to 50%.

00:01:35.525 --> 00:01:40.735
If it goes through a second half life,
it will have 25 percent remaining.

00:01:41.205 --> 00:01:44.065
And that will keep going down over time.

00:01:44.085 --> 00:01:51.364
It will keep decreasing as we move further
and further and further, decaying by half.

00:01:52.065 --> 00:01:54.425
So what kind of problems
could you possibly see?

00:01:54.455 --> 00:01:56.925
Let's go through a bunch
of different calculations.

00:01:57.195 --> 00:02:01.455
to show how this is going to happen and
we'll even look at what kind of ranges of

00:02:01.465 --> 00:02:06.065
values we'll even see for what to expect
when it comes to working with half lives.

00:02:06.275 --> 00:02:07.125
Let's take a look.

00:02:16.454 --> 00:02:18.305
The first thing is what about the ranges?

00:02:18.505 --> 00:02:22.695
So half life values can range from
all kinds of things from minutes

00:02:22.744 --> 00:02:27.065
to years to sometimes millions and
billions of years depending on the

00:02:27.105 --> 00:02:32.154
actual radioactive process and how
stable or unstable the element is.

00:02:32.459 --> 00:02:36.490
Here I just included about four different
examples that we have here, ranging

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:40.669
from carbon 14, which we use to date
historical artifacts that we find

00:02:40.669 --> 00:02:44.959
around the world, to things that could
be used more in medical applications

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.760
where they last on shorter timescales.

00:02:48.269 --> 00:02:52.079
So one thing you might notice is that
they all have different processes such

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.959
as beta particles, alpha particles,
positrons, where they could last

00:02:56.989 --> 00:03:01.480
everywhere from thousands of years
to as short as just a few minutes.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:03.859
So we're going to be talking about
all kinds of different scales

00:03:03.859 --> 00:03:05.470
and just adding up the values.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:08.980
So this is for introductory chemistry,
so we're not going to be going

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:12.539
through the exponential uh, equations
that you'll see in things that

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:16.249
are more general chemistry one or
general chemistry two with kinetics.

00:03:16.609 --> 00:03:19.079
This is just introductory level examples.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:21.579
Let's go into the different problems.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:26.510
With our first example, what we have
here is a 20 mg sample of iodine

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:30.019
131 decays, and it only leaves 2.

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:32.000
5 mg.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:33.840
How long would this take?

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:36.350
How exactly are we going
to go through this?

00:03:36.369 --> 00:03:39.620
Well, the first thing is we have to
remember is that every time we go through

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:45.890
a half life, the mass or the amount is
going to decrease by So, there are a

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:49.440
lot of different little strategies that
we can go through for how to do this.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.329
What we have to remember is
that our original sample is

00:03:52.329 --> 00:03:54.200
starting at 20 milligrams.

00:03:54.739 --> 00:03:57.469
And what we are going to be
doing is dividing it by half

00:03:57.649 --> 00:03:59.499
for every single half life.

00:04:00.039 --> 00:04:01.690
So being that we're going from 20 to 2.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:04.969
5, let's first just
divide our mass in half.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:11.260
So our first one would go from 20 to
10, our second one would go from 10

00:04:11.329 --> 00:04:13.910
to 5, right, 10 divided by 2 is 5.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:17.659
Then if we take 5 divided
by 2, we will get to 2.

00:04:17.889 --> 00:04:18.569
5.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:22.280
So, how much time exactly passed?

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:23.280
Well, let's take a look.

00:04:23.724 --> 00:04:29.105
Every single one of these arrows
represents a half life, so it's in a

00:04:29.105 --> 00:04:31.755
certain amount of time that is passing.

00:04:32.465 --> 00:04:36.665
So in this particular problem,
a half life is equal to 8 days.

00:04:37.225 --> 00:04:39.235
So what exactly occurred?

00:04:39.764 --> 00:04:45.615
Well, after the first half life that
is here, this was 8 days exactly, which

00:04:45.745 --> 00:04:52.110
as the course of time took place, it
went from 100 percent of our sample and

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:57.370
decayed it down to 50 percent across
the course of one single half life.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:03.039
Now, once we went to the second half
life, which was another 8 days, so

00:05:03.039 --> 00:05:10.060
a total of 16, it went from 10 to 5,
meaning that we now have 25 percent

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:12.160
of our original sample remaining.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:17.939
Now, when we go through our last and
final half life, we are down to 12.

00:05:17.989 --> 00:05:25.069
5%, Which means that this took
a total of 24 days for this to

00:05:25.069 --> 00:05:29.689
break down to decompose and go
through this beta decay process.

00:05:30.169 --> 00:05:32.059
So these are some of the
ways you can map it out.

00:05:32.119 --> 00:05:35.030
You don't have to do all the percents and
all the work all the time, but it just

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:36.830
helps to be able to visualize and see it.

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:39.369
Let's go take a look
at some more examples.

00:05:39.579 --> 00:05:43.320
If you start with a hundred
grams of radon, how much sample

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:46.469
would be left after 44 days?

00:05:46.909 --> 00:05:52.049
So, and in this problem I also give
you exactly how long the individual

00:05:52.049 --> 00:05:56.789
half life is, so we can see that
we have a half life of 11 days.

00:05:57.325 --> 00:06:02.645
So what we need to do is map out
exactly how long would this take.

00:06:03.275 --> 00:06:06.575
So this one, it could be, you could
do it a slightly different way.

00:06:07.025 --> 00:06:10.104
Well, how many half lives
would fit into 44 days?

00:06:10.135 --> 00:06:12.504
Well, we can kind of do a
little bit of math here.

00:06:12.834 --> 00:06:19.224
If we have 44 days total, and I
divide that by 11 days, it's going

00:06:19.224 --> 00:06:23.864
to be about four half lives is
how long this is going to take.

00:06:25.135 --> 00:06:28.475
Let's go how that would work, right?

00:06:28.555 --> 00:06:30.965
I would first start
once again at 100 grams.

00:06:31.695 --> 00:06:35.754
Across the first half life,
it would go down to 50 grams.

00:06:36.625 --> 00:06:39.944
The second half life
would go down to 25 grams.

00:06:40.874 --> 00:06:44.294
The third half life would go down to 12.

00:06:44.295 --> 00:06:44.845
5 grams.

00:06:45.785 --> 00:06:49.115
And then the fourth half
life would go down to 6.

00:06:49.165 --> 00:06:50.735
25 grams.

00:06:51.615 --> 00:06:55.525
Now to show, show our work here,
we can put down our t halves,

00:06:55.545 --> 00:07:00.165
just to show that each time this
decayed, this would be changing.

00:07:00.485 --> 00:07:04.415
So every time that this happens,
this is going to be making,

00:07:04.424 --> 00:07:09.994
going from radon and transforming
into a new element as a result.

00:07:10.554 --> 00:07:15.095
And then we can see that it's going
to be releasing alpha particles every

00:07:15.095 --> 00:07:16.985
single time that this is happening.

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:19.070
So it's going to be changing.

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:21.520
So this is how much is decaying.

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:23.950
So at the end, you're only
going to have about 6.

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:28.730
25 percent of your 100 gram
sample that is remaining.

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:32.270
So let's take a look at some other
different types of problems that

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:34.029
we could see when we do these.

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:37.870
If we have a sample of 3, 1 hydrogen.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:39.400
So hydrogen 3.

00:07:39.754 --> 00:07:44.914
And that's decaying through, uh,
beta decay, and it only leaves 3.

00:07:44.914 --> 00:07:47.414
1 percent of the original sample.

00:07:47.914 --> 00:07:49.465
How long would this take?

00:07:49.874 --> 00:07:52.455
The half life for this one is 12.

00:07:52.525 --> 00:07:53.474
3 years.

00:07:54.034 --> 00:07:54.594
How long?

00:07:55.355 --> 00:07:56.175
How can we do it?

00:07:56.734 --> 00:07:59.154
Well, with this one, we weren't,
we didn't get a starting amount,

00:07:59.215 --> 00:08:00.484
but we were given a percent.

00:08:01.075 --> 00:08:04.155
So if we know that every half
life decays by half, why don't we

00:08:04.155 --> 00:08:05.495
just map out the percents, right?

00:08:06.434 --> 00:08:09.235
Just like how we did in the
very beginning of this video.

00:08:09.895 --> 00:08:11.074
So if we have 100%...

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:17.460
It would decay down to 50%, which
would then decay down to 25%,

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:20.600
which would then go down to 12.

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:25.570
5%, and that would
further decay down to 6.

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:29.879
25%, which would then go down to 3.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:32.100
125%, which is approximately
where we want to go.

00:08:32.679 --> 00:08:34.869
So how many, how many
times will this change?

00:08:35.089 --> 00:08:36.459
Well, we can number it out, right?

00:08:36.459 --> 00:08:38.630
We have one, two, three.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:43.510
for five half lives that this
will have to go through in order

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:46.500
for 3, 1 hydrogen to fully decay.

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:51.220
So, all's we need to do is take our
half life value, right, which is 12.

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:52.040
3 years.

00:08:52.555 --> 00:08:56.555
And we would multiply that by
five and we would end up with our

00:08:56.555 --> 00:09:00.615
final answer for this particular
problem, which would be 61.

00:09:00.615 --> 00:09:07.695
5 years is how long this would take for
it to fully go down to only 3 percent

00:09:08.005 --> 00:09:10.535
remaining in this particular problem.

00:09:11.194 --> 00:09:13.544
So let's take a look at another example.

00:09:13.944 --> 00:09:18.145
You start with 200
milligrams of thallium 207.

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:22.399
So after 100 minutes, there is only 12.

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:25.149
5 milligrams left of your sample.

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:29.879
What is the half life
of this decay process?

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:31.990
So how can we try to figure it out?

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:36.639
Well, we were given how much time there
was, and we also have the starting and

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:39.589
the end masses of this particular problem.

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:43.770
So why don't we try to map
out how much we would have.

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:48.939
So the first thing we would do is,
we are starting with 200 milligrams.

00:09:49.369 --> 00:09:53.680
And we are going to be dividing
that mass every single time it goes

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:55.479
through a half life calculation.

00:09:56.069 --> 00:09:59.379
So we'll go from 200 to
100 would be the first one.

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:04.039
Then on the next one we
would go from 100 to 50.

00:10:05.179 --> 00:10:08.919
Then when we divide it again
we're going to go from 50 to 25.

00:10:09.969 --> 00:10:15.469
And then when we go one more time
we're going to go from 25 to 12.

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:15.749
5.

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:20.009
So we are just dividing that
in half with each iteration.

00:10:20.629 --> 00:10:23.199
Now, how, how much is our half life?

00:10:23.209 --> 00:10:24.479
How do we do this?

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:33.310
Well, if you can see, we have 1, 2, 3,
4, our individual half life intervals.

00:10:33.909 --> 00:10:38.010
We know that there was
100 minutes that passed.

00:10:38.380 --> 00:10:40.069
So how would we figure out the half life?

00:10:40.619 --> 00:10:45.439
Well, we're just going to divide it by the
number of intervals, so 100 divided by 4.

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:49.860
And what that's going to tell
you is our half life, which

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:52.320
in this case is 25 minutes.

00:10:52.899 --> 00:10:56.229
It's one half life for
this particular reaction.

00:10:56.879 --> 00:11:01.729
Now, if this were to kind of keep
going over and over and over again, you

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:06.409
could keep showing how far this could
truly decay during that time period.

00:11:06.949 --> 00:11:07.999
It could keep going.

00:11:08.079 --> 00:11:11.089
Now, with half lives, this is a
general idea for most of these

00:11:11.089 --> 00:11:15.719
expressions, being that as they decay,
they're going to continuously keep

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:18.829
approaching 0 percent over time.

00:11:19.420 --> 00:11:23.110
So you can see this keeps dividing
and dividing and dividing.

00:11:23.819 --> 00:11:28.090
Generally, the rule of thumb is
eventually it will get down to just

00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:33.139
one particle left or one little amount
left and it will decay to nothing.

00:11:33.689 --> 00:11:37.540
It usually takes about 10 cycles
or 10 half lives for that to happen

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:39.860
for it to be over 99 percent gone.

00:11:40.210 --> 00:11:42.859
You will see that you can
continue dividing it by two as

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:46.040
long as you want, but this is
how radioactive processes work.

00:11:46.089 --> 00:11:46.869
They take time.

00:11:47.295 --> 00:11:50.064
They're an unstable,
they're metastable, right?

00:11:50.454 --> 00:11:54.104
So as it goes to that radioactive
decay, we will see them continuously

00:11:54.144 --> 00:11:58.444
break apart, transform into
new daughter nuclei as it goes.

